---
title: Run coding agents on a VPS and control them from your browser
description: Keep Codex and pi running on a headless Linux server under systemd, and follow and steer their sessions from any browser with Pontia.
published: "2026-11-11"
---

An always-on Linux server is a good home for coding agents. A long task keeps going when your laptop sleeps, the agent works next to the repository and build tools, and you can check in from any device. What you still need is a way to watch and steer the agent without keeping an SSH session open.

Pontia runs on the server as a systemd user service and serves a Dashboard you open in a browser. This guide sets up a VPS or any headless Linux machine so that Codex or pi sessions survive logouts and you can control them from a phone.

## What you get

- Agents run on the server, with its files, tools, and credentials. Nothing executes in Pontia Cloud.
- The Pontia service starts with your user's systemd manager and restarts on failure.
- The server needs no open inbound ports. It connects out to an Edge relay, and the browser reaches it through that relay over an end-to-end encrypted session.
- You start, follow, interrupt, and continue sessions at [app.pontia.dev](https://app.pontia.dev) from a phone or another computer.

## Requirements

- x86_64 or ARM64 Linux with GNU userspace.
- A working systemd user manager for the account that will run the agents.
- The agents you plan to use: the `codex` CLI, or `pi` and `tmux`.

Run everything below as the same non-root user. Pontia uses that user's home directory, agent credentials, and systemd user service.

## 1. Prepare the user session

On a server, the systemd user manager normally stops when your last SSH session closes, and user services stop with it. Enable lingering so they keep running:

```sh
loginctl enable-linger "$(id -un)"
systemctl --user is-system-running
```

`is-system-running` should report `running` (or `degraded` if an unrelated unit failed). If it cannot connect to the user manager, log in again over SSH so systemd starts it.

Selecting Codex in `pontia init` enables lingering for you. For pi-only setups, run the command above yourself.

## 2. Sign in to your agents

Authenticate each agent as this user. Codex supports a device code flow that works over SSH:

```sh
codex login --device-auth
```

For pi, configure your model provider credentials as described in the [pi quickstart](https://pi.dev/docs/latest/quickstart).

## 3. Install and initialize Pontia

```sh
curl -fsSL https://get.pontia.dev/install.sh | sh
pontia init
```

The installer places `pontia` and `pontiad` in `$HOME/.local/bin`; add it to your `PATH` if needed. In the initializer, select pi, Codex, or both. Pontia then:

1. installs `~/.config/systemd/user/pontia.service` and starts it;
2. for Codex, installs and enables `pontia-codex.service`, which starts the Codex app-server daemon;
3. signs you in to Pontia Cloud and registers the server for remote access.

The server has no browser, so the sign-in step prints a verification URL and a one-time code. Open the URL on your phone or laptop, sign in with Google or GitHub, and approve the code. Then choose an Edge and a device name.

To script the setup, or to have an agent run it step by step, use [non-interactive initialization](/docs/initialization#non-interactive-initialization).

## 4. Open the Dashboard

Go to [app.pontia.dev](https://app.pontia.dev) and select the server. Activate a project directory as a Workspace and start a session. By default the Workspace Browser shows your home directory; see [Workspaces](/docs/workspaces) to limit it to specific roots.

## Operate the service

Use the Pontia CLI for day-to-day management:

```sh
pontia status
pontia doctor
pontia update
```

`pontia doctor` checks the service, configuration, login, and remote registration, and suggests repairs. `pontia update` installs the latest signed release and restarts the service. For lower-level diagnosis, the usual systemd tools work:

```sh
systemctl --user status pontia.service --no-pager --full
journalctl --user -u pontia.service --no-pager -n 200
```

pi sessions run inside tmux, outside the Pontia service, so restarting or updating Pontia does not stop them. To use the native pi TUI on a session the Dashboard started, SSH in and attach to it. Its tmux session name starts with `pontia_`:

```sh
tmux ls
tmux attach -t SESSION_NAME
```

## Long-running work

Agents on a server can work for hours. Two Pontia features help with that:

- Sessions are durable. Close the browser and come back later from another device; the conversation and controls are still there.
- [Workflows](/docs/workflows) split a large task into stages, each run by a separate agent session with explicit handoff files. You follow each stage from the Dashboard, and an agent that hits an unexpected problem can request a revised plan.

## Security notes

The local Dashboard listens on `127.0.0.1:8080` by default. Remote access goes through the encrypted relay, so you do not need to expose that port. If you change `bind_addr` to a public address, you expose a token-authenticated API to that network and must protect it yourself.

Each agent keeps its own sandbox and approval settings. Pontia does not add an operating-system sandbox, so treat the server account as the boundary for what agents can touch. [Security](/docs/security) covers credentials and incident response.

If you want the relay on infrastructure you control, [deploy a self-hosted Edge](/docs/self-hosted-edge) on a public Linux server.

